<Base>
  <section class="hero" id="top">
    <img class="hero-media" src={img.chain} alt="Lake country winding through pine forest at dusk" />
    <div class="hero-copy wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow reveal">A history of the crossing</p>
      <h1 class="reveal delay-1">Railroad town. Lake town. Race town.</h1>
      <p class="lede reveal delay-2">Before the shops and the resorts there was a Gull Lake village and a name the maps still carry: Bagone-giizhig — Hole-in-the-Day. Brainerd stayed because the chain turned a weekend into a century of family cabins, a racetrack in the pines, and a city that appears on the ice every January.</p>
      <p class="scroll-cue">Scroll the century</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="marquee" aria-hidden="true">
    <div class="marquee-track">{[...years, ...years].map((y) => <span>{y} *</span>)}</div>
  </div>

  <section class="chapter paper" id="origins">
    <span class="chapter-num">01</span>
    <div class="wrap split">
      <div>
        <p class="kicker">Chapter I</p>
        <h2>A river already named</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>The land that became Brainerd was Anishinaabe — Ojibwe — territory long before a railroad needed a crossing. Woodland peoples left burial mounds at what is now the Gull Lake Dam. Ojibwe families took wild rice, maple sugar, and fish from the chain the maps would later sell as vacation country.</p>
          <p>The Ojibwe name for Gull is remembered as <em>Ga-gaiashkonzikag sagaiigun</em> — the lake of the young gulls. Crow Wing Village, about ten miles south at the meeting of the Crow Wing and the Mississippi, was the earlier town: traders, mixed-blood families, a Catholic mission, and the agency that treated with the Mississippi band.</p>
          <p>Zebulon Pike camped near the future townsite on Christmas Day, 1805. What changed the map was not discovery. It was a railroad that needed to cross the river north of Crow Wing.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <figure class="photo">
        <img src={img.pines} alt="Pine forest in northern light" />
        <figcaption class="caption">The city-of-pines before the shops — and before the shops, a village on Gull.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter" id="hole">
    <span class="chapter-num">02</span>
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">Bagone-giizhig · Gull Lake</p>
      <h2>Hole-in-the-Day</h2>
      <div class="prose" style="max-width:46rem">
        <p>The English name on the bay is a rough translation of Bagone-giizhig — also rendered Hole-in-the-Sky, a hole made in the day or the heavens. There were two chiefs of that name, father and son. The bay, a small nearby lake, and a road by Nisswa remember them. Most people meet the name now as a place to drill ice in January.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="grid-2" style="margin-top:2.2rem">
        <article class="card">
          <p class="meta">The Elder · c. 1800–1847</p>
          <h3>A village on Gull</h3>
          <p>Bagone-giizhig the Elder was born on Madeline Island in Lake Superior. He was Bear clan, not a hereditary civil chief in the old pattern, and he made a different kind of power: war reputation, Midewiwin standing, and a talent for talking to Americans as if he spoke for a region, not a single village.</p>
          <p>Around 1836 he moved his band from Sandy Lake to the Gull Lake country and founded the Mississippi Ojibwe community that treaty-makers would treat as central. Families kept a sugar bush between Gull and Round and North Long. Crow Wing, downriver, became the mixed town where that power met the fur trade.</p>
          <p>In May 1847, coming home from a council at Prairie du Chien, he fell from a wagon at the Mississippi crossing at Little Falls. The wheel crushed his chest. The name, and the claim that went with it, passed to his son.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <p class="meta">The Younger · c. 1825–1868</p>
          <h3>Gwiiwizens, then the name</h3>
          <p>The son was born Gwiiwizens — Boy. He was about twenty when the Elder died. At Fond du Lac that same year he stood up in a treaty council and told the room he was first chief of all Minnesota Ojibwe. Many leaders never forgave the claim. The United States often preferred to have one man to sign for.</p>
          <p>He kept a farm and a two-story house near Gull and Crow Wing. He sat in the treaties that carved the country into lumber and reservation lines, including the 1855 agreement that reserved a Gull Lake homeland and the 1867 treaty that created White Earth. He told people not to move until the government built what it had promised.</p>
          <p>In 1862, while the U.S.–Dakota War burned the Minnesota River valley, he threatened the Crow Wing agency and Fort Ripley. The Gull Lake mission burned. Captives were taken and released. Pillager leaders from Leech Lake refused to follow him into a general war. Councils at Crow Wing pulled the crisis back from the edge. The suspicion never left.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="wrap split" style="margin-top:3rem">
      <div>
        <p class="kicker">June 27, 1868</p>
        <h2>The road between Gull and Crow Wing</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>That morning he left for Washington to fight the removal written into the White Earth treaty. His cousin and bodyguard, a man named Ojibwe, drove. Near the Gull River outlet above Crow Wing — local memory puts it by today’s Fisherman’s Bridge — Leech Lake Pillager men stopped the buggy.</p>
          <p>He stood and called that they had caught him unarmed. He was shot, then killed on the road. The party looted the Gull River farm. He was buried at the Catholic ground at Crow Wing. Descendants later said the body was moved in secret so the grave would not be robbed. No one was prosecuted.</p>
          <p>For decades the story was jealousy, or payback for 1862, or anger that he tried to keep mixed-blood traders off White Earth. Anton Treuer’s later work, and testimony the killers themselves gave years after, points to a hired killing arranged by Crow Wing traders who feared losing their place in the new reservation economy. Crow Wing Village faded. Two years later the railroad crossed the Mississippi a few miles north and named a different town.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <figure class="photo">
        <img src={img.lake} alt="Northern lake shore lined with pines" />
        <figcaption class="caption">Gull Lake country — village, sugar bush, and later the bay that keeps the English name.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter paper" id="rails">
    <span class="chapter-num">03</span>
    <div class="wrap split">
      <figure class="photo">
        <img src={img.steam} alt="Steam locomotive at a historic station" />
        <figcaption class="caption">A town built so locomotives could cross and be repaired.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div>
        <p class="kicker">Chapter III · 1870</p>
        <h2>Named in a boardroom</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>Northern Pacific president John Gregory Smith chose a crossing seven miles north of Crow Wing. On October 1, 1870, directors named it Brainerd. The wooden trestle opened March 8, 1871. By the 1920s nine in ten households lived off the railroad shops. When the shops shrank, the lakes had to become the payroll.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="film" aria-label="Photographs of the lakes and town">
    <figure>
      <img src={img.tower} alt="Brainerd historic concrete water tower" />
      <figcaption class="caption">The 1918–21 water tower — Paul Bunyan's flashlight</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure>
      <img src={img.chain} alt="Connected lakes winding through pine forest" />
      <figcaption class="caption">The Gull Lake chain look — water folded into the pines</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure>
      <img src={img.dock} alt="Wooden dock on a northern lake" />
      <figcaption class="caption">Resort light on a north-country shore</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure>
