      <figcaption class="caption">The same water in January — Ice Extravaganza weather</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter" id="lakes">
    <span class="chapter-num">04</span>
    <div class="wrap split">
      <div>
        <p class="kicker">The chain</p>
        <h2>Gull Lake is why people stay</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>More than 460 lakes sit within 25 miles of Brainerd. The one that made the letterhead is Gull — about 9,947 acres, 80 feet at the deepest hole, 38 miles of shore, chained to a 13,000-acre system that cabin families memorize like streets.</p>
          <p>The chain is Gull and Upper Gull, Round, Love, Roy, Margaret, Spring, Bass, plus Steamboat Bay and Wilson's Bay. Nisswa and Spider get counted depending on who is holding the map. The Corps raised the water when Gull Lake Dam went in around 1912–13. More than 2,500 homes now face that water.</p>
          <p>Hole-in-the-Day Bay keeps the Younger’s English name. In summer it is docks and golf. In January, if the ice will hold, it becomes a temporary city of 10,000 anglers — a gathering on water that was a homeland first.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <figure class="photo">
        <img src={img.chain} alt="Aerial-looking view of a pine-ringed lake chain" />
        <figcaption class="caption">Eight lakes and two bays. Gull is the big water; the rest are how you get lost on purpose.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter paper" id="resorts">
    <span class="chapter-num">05</span>
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">Neighbors on the peninsula</p>
      <h2>The Maddens and the Craguns</h2>
      <div class="prose" style="max-width:46rem">
        <p>Two University of Minnesota advertising-desk friends — Jack Madden and Merrill Cragun Sr. — helped invent Brainerd as a vacation. In 1936 they put a giant lumberjack in a parade. Then they built resorts within waving distance on Gull Lake and spent the rest of the century arguing, in the nicest way, over who had the better week.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="grid-2" style="margin-top:2.4rem">
        <article class="card">
          <img class="card-photo" src={img.golf} alt="Golf fairway through trees" />
          <p class="meta">Madden's on Gull Lake · since 1929</p>
          <h3>Nothing down, ten years to pay</h3>
          <p>The peninsula starts with timber money. T. H. Harrison planned a resort there as early as 1909. After his death his son John partnered with Kansas City developer Chester Start. They built a golf course and the Pine Beach Hotel just in time for the Crash.</p>
          <p>Jack Madden summered at that hotel in 1931. In 1932 he and his uncle Tom leased the failing course for $500. They could not always make the lease. Harrison and Start were desperate enough to sell with no money down. Jack wired that he had no cash. They sold it anyway.</p>
          <p>Brother Jim came north to caddy and stay. Mission Point began as three cabins. The Madden Inn in 1934 was a lunch counter and a caddy shack with no guest rooms. By 1941 the brothers were sole partners, with a couple dozen cabins on the point. A 1964 fire took the clubhouse and the new O'Madden Pub on opening night. They rebuilt.</p>
          <p>Jim's daughter Deb and her husband Brian Thuringer took the keys in the 1970s. Their children Ben and Abbey grew up serving coffee from a 12-foot boat and working the course. Fourth generation, same 1,000-acre peninsula, still independent.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <img class="card-photo" src={img.resort} alt="Lakeside resort lodge among trees" />
          <p class="meta">Cragun's Resort · 1940–2025 in the family</p>
          <h3>A swamp, a gamble</h3>
          <p>Merrill K. Cragun Sr. and Louise registered Paul Bunyan Inc. in 1934 to promote the lakes. The war interrupted. In 1940 they bought seven acres on a hill that, Dutch later said, sloped down to a marsh on Steamboat Bay. A handful of cabins went up. The original lodge was finished in 1945. By the end of 1947 they had a lodge and twelve cabins — one of the first true Gull Lake resorts.</p>
          <p>Louise ran the place with father-in-law Virgil while Merrill kept a Twin Cities job and came up on weekends. Season: Memorial Day to Labor Day. Dining room for twenty in 1948. Six motel rooms in 1949. Dutch's first job, age nine, was worms and minnows.</p>
          <p>Dutch took over in 1957. He met Irma at a dance in San Francisco, married her in 1965, and they turned a summer camp into a year-round house: pool in 1963, enclosed by 1977, Island View absorbed in 1988, 54 holes of golf, nearly a mile of shore. In 2016 they built a home across the lake so they could look back at what they had made.</p>
          <p>In March 2025, at 93, Dutch sold to Leisure Hotels and Resorts of Baxter — reported as one of the largest resort deals in Minnesota history. Eighty-five family years. The sign still says Gull Lake.</p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <figure class="photo" style="margin-top:1.8rem;min-height:320px">
        <img src={img.cabin} alt="Snowy cabin among pines on a northern lake" style="min-height:320px" />
        <figcaption class="caption">The product they sold in 1936 is still the product: water, pine, and a week that feels longer than it is.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter" id="bir">
    <span class="chapter-num">06</span>
    <div class="wrap split">
      <div>
        <p class="kicker">Donnybrooke · 1968</p>
        <h2>A racetrack in the jack pine</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>George Montgomery was a retired Northwest Airlines pilot with a Shelby 427 Cobra and nowhere nearby to race it. He bought parcels south of North Long Lake and cut a 3.1-mile, ten-turn road course through the trees. He named it Donnybrooke for Donny Skogmo and Brooke Kinnard, two Minnesota road racers killed at Road America.</p>
          <p>Opening day, July 1968: no permanent restrooms, no grandstands, no safety barriers, a small timing tower. The first official event was an NHRA drag race on the mile straight. Doc Halladay won Top Fuel. Can-Am arrived in 1970–72 — Denny Hulme, Peter Revson, François Cevert. USAC Indy cars came through; Gordon Johncock and Dan Gurney each won a heat in 1969.</p>
          <p>Weather, thin crowds, and unfinished infrastructure put the place in foreclosure by March 1973. Jerry Hansen — the winningest amateur in SCCA history — bought it with Dick Roe and renamed it Brainerd International Raceway. Paul Newman set a lap record here in 1977 and won his first professional race on this pavement in 1982.</p>
          <p>That same year BIR installed bleachers salvaged from Metropolitan Stadium and hosted the first Quaker State NorthStar Nationals. More than 50,000 people came. Shirley Muldowney beat Gary Beck in Top Fuel. The campground known as the Zoo became its own folklore. Donald Williamson owned the track in the 1990s; the Copham family bought it in 2006 so the pines would not become lots. The NHRA Nationals still shake August.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <figure class="photo">
        <img src={img.cars} alt="Sports car on an open stretch of pavement" />
        <figcaption class="caption">A 3.1-mile road course and a quarter-mile drag strip on the same property in the pines.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
    <div class="wrap" style="margin-top:2rem">
      <div class="stat-row">
        <div class="stat"><b>3.1 mi</b><span>Original Donnybrooke road course</span></div>
        <div class="stat"><b>50k</b><span>Fans at the first NHRA nationals</span></div>
        <div class="stat"><b>1977</b><span>Newman lap record</span></div>
        <div class="stat"><b>2006</b><span>Cophams keep the track</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter paper" id="ice">
    <span class="chapter-num">07</span>
    <div class="wrap split">
      <figure class="photo">
        <img src={img.ice} alt="Vast frozen northern lake under winter sky" />
        <figcaption class="caption">Hole-in-the-Day Bay in January — a city that lasts three hours.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div>
        <p class="kicker">Gull Lake · every January</p>
        <h2>The Ice Fishing Extravaganza</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>In 1990 a few Brainerd Jaycees, looking for a way to put people on the lakes in the dead month, sketched a contest on the side of a beer case. Chris Dalbec pushed the first running in January 1991. Nobody planned on the world's largest charitable ice-fishing tournament.</p>
          <p>Volunteers drill thousands of holes on Hole-in-the-Day Bay. More than 10,000 anglers walk out. Gates open in the morning; the official bite is often just three hours. For that window the county's largest city is on the ice. Shuttles leave from BIR. When Gull is too thin — it has happened more than once — the contest jumps to Round Lake or runs a hybrid.</p>
          <p>Most of the money goes to Confidence Learning Center and a web of local nonprofits. Organizers put the charitable total above $3 million since the start, plus about a million a year in winter business for the town. The first winning fish, in 1991, was Ken Geiszler's 6.83-pound walleye.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="wrap" style="margin-top:2.2rem">
      <figure class="photo" style="min-height:280px">
        <img src={img.iceCrowd} alt="Winter gathering on a snow-covered northern landscape" style="min-height:280px" />
        <figcaption class="caption">The look of the tournament weekend: a white plain, a tree line, and more people than the town holds in July.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter" id="events">
